[Music]  g is for greenwash they are basically  painting a story  like they're doing what needs to be done  for the climate and ecological emergency  there'll be a big greenwashing effort in  glasgow that needs to be called out and  recognized  [Music]  there's two problems two things i'll  focus on here one of them is is claims  of being carbon neutral so today we  often see that high emitting companies  claim that their businesses or perhaps  their products are already carbon  neutral and that we see as a real red  flag  because what it is unfortunately it's in  fictional accounting for carbon what  companies call carbon offsetting but the  problem that we have is that carbon  offsets don't actually cancel out the  emissions from fossil fuels  and they can't critically  replace what we need to do which is to  drastically reduce burning coal oil and  gas companies can't use carbon offsets  to reassure consumers that they're  actually acting on climate change but  they nevertheless often just label it  carbon neutral  there are more and more companies  putting out promises of reaching net  zero by 2050.  we think the easiest way to tell if that  is credible is just to look to see if  it's clearly backed up by immediate  action  the science here is clear it says we  need to reduce emissions by at least  half by 2030 that's in the next nine  years so the question we'd ask is why  isn't a company with a net zero target  talking about what percentage of its  overall emissions it's reducing in the  next three years in the next five years  in that sort of time frame  [Applause]  companies particularly oil and gas  companies have  some emissions that come from their  their operations but the vast vast  majority of their emissions come from  their products  and so when they're not being completely  clear about the amount of emissions that  they're going to cut from their overall  emissions and the including those of  their products then you get a real risk  of green washing so we see that green  washing is creeping well outside the oil  and gas industry so this is the key  transition areas things like energy  things like cars planes home heating  meat and also of course the finance  sector that supports them  although we see that some environmental  marketing claims are legitimate many do  give an impression which isn't backed up  by action we can't use any more empty  promises of climate action or reassuring  messages we need real action on real  emissions to reduce them in line with  paris agreement goals  [Music]

